# apps/api skeleton — ready for module development

This file's presence is the signal the "subscriptions" agent polls for
before writing its own modules (subscriptions, licenses, payments, coupons,
plans, bans, flags + their admin routes + the jobs
subscriptions.expire/licenses.revalidate/abuse.scan). Everything below this
line has been built and typechecks (`pnpm --filter @sl/api typecheck`).

## Boot & layout

- `src/config/env.ts` — zod-validated env (`loadEnv()`), memoised. Includes
  every var your module needs (STRIPE_*, ENTITLEMENT_SIGNING_KEY /
  ENTITLEMENT_PUBLIC_KEY) even though this agent doesn't consume them.
- `src/app.ts` — `buildApp(options?)` builds (does not `.listen()`) the
  Fastify instance. Used by `server.ts`, every integration test (`.inject()`),
  and `src/scripts/generate-openapi.ts`.
- `src/server.ts` — real process entrypoint (`pnpm --filter @sl/api dev` /
  `start`).
- `src/worker.ts` — BullMQ job runner, separate process (`pnpm --filter
  @sl/api worker`).
- `src/plugins/*` — config, otel, security (helmet), cors, cookie, csrf,
  request-id, db, redis, entitlements, mailer, metrics, rate-limit, auth,
  websocket, error-handler. Registered in that order in `app.ts`. **Do not
  edit these or `app.ts`'s plugin registration / module-autoload block** —
  if you need an env var or an additive plugin change, extend
  `config/env.ts` additively and say so in your final report; don't touch
  registration order.

## Module convention (mandatory — how to add a module)

Each module is one folder: `src/modules/<name>/index.ts` default-exports a
`fastify-plugin`-wrapped plugin. `app.ts` uses `@fastify/autoload` on
`src/modules`; **autoload registers only `index.ts` per folder** (its own
rule: a directory containing an index file registers *only* that file —
your `service.ts`/`repo.ts`/`schema.ts`/`__tests__/` files next to it are
never auto-registered, they're just imported by `index.ts`).

- **Every route uses its own full absolute path** —
  `fastify.get('/api/v1/subscriptions/me', ...)`, never a relative path
  under an assumed prefix. This was a deliberate finding, not a stylistic
  choice: `fastify-plugin` (`fp()`) marks a plugin as **non-encapsulated**
  so it can decorate/reach the shared instance (`fastify.db`,
  `fastify.authenticate`, etc.) directly — and Fastify's `prefix`
  register-option only applies to *encapsulated* children, so it has **no
  effect** on an fp()-wrapped plugin (verified empirically while building
  this skeleton — autoload's folder-name-derived prefix and any
  `autoPrefix` export are silently ignored for fp() plugins). Don't rely on
  either; always write the complete path.
- Your admin routes belong in their own folders — `admin-subscriptions`,
  `admin-payments`, `admin-coupons`, `admin-plans`, `admin-bans`,
  `admin-flags` (or similar; your call) — each registering full paths under
  `/api/v1/admin/...`. This agent's admin folders are named `admin-users`,
  `admin-audit`, `admin-system`, `admin-toggles`, `admin-config`,
  `admin-activity`, so there's no collision either way.
- Example skeleton:

  ```ts
  // src/modules/subscriptions/index.ts
  import fp from 'fastify-plugin';
  import type { FastifyInstance } from 'fastify';

  export default fp(
    async function subscriptionsModule(fastify: FastifyInstance) {
      fastify.get('/api/v1/subscriptions/me', { onRequest: [fastify.authenticate] }, async (request) => {
        // ...
      });
    },
    { name: 'module:subscriptions', dependencies: ['auth', 'db', 'redis'] },
  );
  ```

- Use `zod` schemas from `@sl/shared` (or module-local ones) as Fastify route
  `schema.body`/`schema.response` — `fastify-type-provider-zod` is already
  wired via `setValidatorCompiler`/`setSerializerCompiler` in `app.ts`, and
  `@fastify/swagger` picks the schemas up for OpenAPI automatically. **For
  `request.body`/`request.query` to come out correctly typed** (not
  `unknown`), call `const app = fastify.withTypeProvider<ZodTypeProvider>();`
  once at the top of your plugin function and register routes on `app.post`/
  `app.get`/etc instead of `fastify.post`/... (same instance, just a
  differently-typed view — `app.db`, `app.authenticate`, etc. all still work
  identically). Every module in this codebase, including this agent's,
  follows this pattern — copy it from `src/modules/auth/index.ts`.

## Getting db / redis / auth / permissions / audit in a route

- `fastify.db` — Drizzle client (`@sl/db`'s `schema`), `fastify.sqlClient` —
  raw `postgres.js` connection (LISTEN/NOTIFY, `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW`).
- `fastify.redis` — general-purpose ioredis client (cache/locks/dedupe).
  `fastify.redisSub` is a **separate** connection reserved for pub/sub
  (ws/publish.ts + ws/router.ts use it) — never issue non-pub/sub commands
  on it.
- `fastify.authenticate` — preHandler; verifies bearer or `sl_at` cookie,
  sets `request.authUser = { id, role, plan, sessionId, deviceId }`. Use it
  on every route that requires *any* logged-in user (admin or not).
- `fastify.requireAdmin` — preHandler; `authenticate` + `role === 'admin'`.
- `fastify.requirePermission(permission)` — preHandler factory;
  `requireAdmin` + checks the admin's `admin_role` against
  `@sl/shared`'s `PERMISSION_MATRIX` for that `Permission`. Use this for
  every admin mutation (`users.ban`, `subscriptions.write`, etc.).
- `fastify.verifyCsrf` — preHandler (callback-style, matches
  `fastify.csrfProtection`'s own signature); add it alongside
  `fastify.authenticate` on cookie-session-mutating dashboard routes. It's a
  no-op when the request carries a bearer `Authorization` header (the
  extension), since bearer calls can't be CSRF'd.
- `recordAudit({ db, actor, action, entityType, entityId, before, after, ip,
  userAgent, requestId })` from `src/lib/audit.ts` — call this after every
  admin mutation (or any mutation you want in the audit trail). `entityId`
  must be the row's UUID `id` (audit_logs.entity_id is a `uuid` column,
  polymorphic via `entityType`, no FK). It computes the before/after diff
  for you.
- `src/lib/errors.ts` — `AppError` + `AppErrors.*` helpers
  (`AppErrors.notFound('subscription')`, `AppErrors.conflict(...)`, etc.).
  Throw these from anywhere in a request; `plugins/error-handler.ts` renders
  the `{ code, message, details?, requestId }` envelope automatically. Codes
  come from `@sl/shared`'s `ERROR_CODES` — `LICENSE_INVALID`,
  `LICENSE_EXPIRED`, `LICENSE_REVOKED`, `SUBSCRIPTION_REQUIRED`,
  `TRIAL_ABUSE_DETECTED`, `KILL_SWITCH_ACTIVE` are already declared there
  for your module to use (`new AppError('LICENSE_EXPIRED', '...')`).
- `src/lib/ids.ts` — `newId()` (uuidv7) for every app-generated PK.
- `src/lib/pagination.ts` — `encodeCursor`/`decodeCursor`/`paginate()` for
  cursor-based list endpoints (`{ items, nextCursor }`).
- `src/lib/crypto.ts` — `hashSecret`/`verifySecret` (argon2id),
  `randomToken`, `fastHash` (SHA-256 hex, for fast-lookup token hashes),
  `generateLicenseKey()` (Crockford base32 `SL-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX` +
  prefix — already implemented here since it's a pure crypto/formatting
  helper; your licenses module can use it directly instead of
  reimplementing).
- `src/lib/entitlements.ts` — the `EntitlementProvider` interface +
  `DefaultEntitlementProvider` (reads subscriptions/plans/licenses directly,
  signs with `ENTITLEMENT_SIGNING_KEY` via `fastify.entitlements`). This is
  **your** seam to extend: `modules/extension`'s bootstrap/heartbeat routes
  only ever call `fastify.entitlements.getEntitlements(userId)` /
  `.signEntitlementBlob(...)`, never a concrete class. If you need richer
  behaviour (grace periods, etc.), either extend
  `DefaultEntitlementProvider` and redecorate `entitlements` from your own
  module-scoped child context, or ask — don't edit
  `plugins/entitlements.ts` itself (owned by this agent).
- `src/ws/publish.ts` — `publishToUser(fastify.redis, userId, event)` /
  `publishAdmin(fastify.redis, event)`. `event` must match `@sl/shared`'s
  `wsEventSchema` (`subscription.changed` is already in there for you).
- `src/lib/mailer.ts` / `fastify.mailer.send({ to, subject, html, text })` —
  real SMTP in production, in-memory JSON transport in dev/test
  (`fastify.mailer.sentEmails` for test assertions).

## Jobs convention

`src/jobs/<name>.job.ts` default-exports `defineJob({ name, schedule?,
processor })` from `src/jobs/types.ts` (`schedule` is a cron string; omit it
for a job only ever triggered by `queue.add` from elsewhere). `src/worker.ts`
discovers every `*.job.ts` file in `src/jobs/` via `loadJobs()`
(`src/jobs/index.ts` — plain filesystem scan, not `@fastify/autoload`, since
jobs aren't Fastify plugins) and wires each to its own BullMQ `Queue` +
`Worker`. Add `src/jobs/subscriptions.expire.job.ts`,
`src/jobs/licenses.revalidate.job.ts`, `src/jobs/abuse.scan.job.ts` the same
way this agent added `profits.rollup.job.ts` etc. — no registration step
needed beyond dropping the file in.

```ts
// src/jobs/subscriptions.expire.job.ts
import { defineJob } from './types.js';

export default defineJob({
  name: 'subscriptions.expire',
  schedule: '*/5 * * * *',
  async processor(_job, { db, log }) {
    // ...
  },
});
```

## Tests

Integration tests live in `src/modules/<name>/__tests__/*.test.ts`
(`vitest.config.ts` includes `src/**/__tests__/*.test.ts`, runs against the
real `TEST_DATABASE_URL` Postgres and real local Redis, `fileParallelism:
false` since files share test-DB state). Use `@sl/db`'s
`resetDatabase(db)`/`migrateTestDatabase()` (already applied once via
`src/test/global-setup.ts`) between tests, and `buildApp()` +
`app.inject({ method, url, payload, headers })` — see
`src/modules/auth/__tests__/register.test.ts` for the pattern (build app
once per file in `beforeAll`, `resetDatabase` in `beforeEach`). For Redis,
either reuse `app.redis` with a per-test-run key prefix you flush in
`afterAll`, or connect your own `ioredis` client the same way.

## Env additions

None yet beyond what's already in `config/env.ts` (all your `STRIPE_*` /
`ENTITLEMENT_*` vars are already declared there, optional, so your module
never has to touch this file either — just read `fastify.config.STRIPE_*`).
If you truly need a new one, add it there additively (new field, safe
default or `.optional()`) and mention it in your final report.

## Package.json / deps

This agent's `apps/api/package.json` is done; add your own deps (`stripe`,
etc.) with `pnpm add --filter @sl/api <pkg>` — expect `package.json` and the
root `pnpm-lock.yaml` to change under you as a result of your own installs;
that's expected and not a conflict to resolve against this agent's work.

